Notebook[{
Cell["Tropical Algebra of ReLU Networks", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"2368d4da-c8f5-4604-9899-3c107736339b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["A ReLU neural network is literally a tropical rational map.",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " We take that statement seriously. First we set up the max-plus (tropical) \
semiring from scratch and run it through three classical applications -- \
shortest paths, project scheduling, and Karp's eigenvalue. Then we show that \
a 1-hidden-layer ReLU classifier is a difference of two tropical polynomials, \
derive that fact symbolically on small examples, and finally train an actual \
classifier in WL and display its decision boundary as a tropical hypersurface."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"caeff2da-2bdd-4816-b42e-4b1993062584"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Companion code lives at ",
 StyleBox["github.com/mthiel74/TropicalAlgebravsReLU",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"39fef680-2a9a-4d04-9e55-363c315ed9af"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Reference: L. Zhang, G. Naitzat, L.-H. Lim, ",
 StyleBox["Tropical Geometry of Deep Neural Networks",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ICML 2018."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3790c7dc-262c-4dab-8155-756e4acb23d5"],

Cell["1.  Max-plus algebra in 60 seconds", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b5f30e46-af05-4a75-bfa0-d8cb98401d05"],

Cell["\<\
The tropical (max-plus) semiring is the set of real numbers extended with \
negative infinity, equipped with two operations that look like the addition \
and multiplication of classical algebra but are actually:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"55b4bae6-ec88-4cdc-bd9a-e51e8b1b025b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "  ",
 StyleBox["a \[CirclePlus] b := Max[a, b]",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 "         (the tropical \"sum\")\n",
 "  ",
 StyleBox["a \[CircleTimes] b := a + b",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 "          (the tropical \"product\")"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"eb8b3860-2621-437f-b2a5-828f314039d7"],

Cell["\<\
The identities flip from {0, 1} to {-Infinity, 0}. Apart from the lack of \
additive inverses (you cannot tropically subtract universally) all the \
familiar axioms hold.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"86a21b6b-13d6-4578-a51f-0adb7d35ad76"],

