Cell["\<\
Figure 6. The bivariate tropical polynomial p(x, y) = max(0, x, y, x + y - 1) \
has four pieces (left) and Newton polytope the unit square (right).\
\>", "Text",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Helvetica",
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,ExpressionUUID->"ea728145-184b-4ba6-98b6-2b4e05389668"],

Cell["6.  Tropical eigenvalues - Karp's algorithm", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"ba11526c-bc85-47b3-919d-0e1d60d9c4b6"],

Cell["\<\
For a square tropical matrix A the role of the eigenvalue is played by the \
MAX MEAN CYCLE WEIGHT of the associated weighted digraph:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"de6c8471-6969-44be-bd2e-0d69fe11d16a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "  ",
 StyleBox["\\[Lambda]_max(A) = max over directed cycles C of  (sum of edge \
weights in C) / (length of C).",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]]
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"599e2190-c1a7-4ac5-95ae-dc8bd7b0485c"],

Cell["\<\
This is the unique tropical eigenvalue when A is irreducible. Karp's \
algorithm computes it in O(n^3) by tracking maximum-weight paths of every \
length up to n.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d0431fca-41fa-43c9-823b-f914dcab1ba0"],

